// B6a — an i32-keyed map DELIVERED to a container position. The map's own rep is settled
// (D1748 made a literal-union key hash as i32, so `{[i32]: V}` is a map like any other at the
// direct spelling), and every position the emitter names — binding, parameter, return,
// `| null`, an array element, a closure result, a struct field, a union struct arm — lowers.
// What was refused was a SPELLING gate (`i32MapSpellingLowerable`) that peeled one `[]` and
// one top-level arrow and rejected everything else, including four positions whose STRING-keyed
// twin runs.
//
// The template's job is to say which of the ~26 delivery positions the i32-keyed map is
// actually served at, so the gate can be narrowed to what is measured rather than to what a
// hand-written cell happened to cover. A SILENT cell here is the gate earning its keep.
//
// The PROOF writes through the delivered map and reads back, because a container that hands
// back a map with the wrong struct reads a plausible length and traps on the write.
//
// Format: scripts/capability-probes/matrix.py, whose docstring is the spec.
// @@PRELUDE@@
function mkMap(): {[i32]: f64} {
  const m: {[i32]: f64} = Map()
  m.set(1, 2.5)
  return m
}
// @@VALUE@@
mkMap()
// @@TYPE@@
{[i32]: f64}
// @@PROOF@@
v.set(2, 3.5)
print(v.get(2) ?? 0.0)
// @@WANT@@
3.5
// @@VALUE2@@
mkMap()
// @@TYPE2@@
{[i32]: f64}
// @@PROOF2@@
print(v.length)
// @@WANT2@@
1
